@title How the API rate limiter works
@subtitle A Redis-backed token bucket in TypeScript, read once
@author Platform team
@date 2026-10-19

! In one paragraph: every caller (tenant plus route) owns a bucket that holds up to 60 tokens and refills at 1 token per second. Each request takes one token. If the bucket has a token, the request goes through; if not, it gets a 429 with a Retry-After. The whole check runs as one Lua script inside Redis, so concurrent requests cannot double-spend a token.

kpi Capacity | 60 tokens | burst size | neutral
kpi Refill | 1 token/s | 60 per minute steady | neutral
kpi Redis timeout | 25 ms | then fail open | neutral
kpi Idle expiry | 120 s | bucket resets to full | neutral

## The flow

Read this diagram top to bottom. The diamond is the only decision; everything else is bookkeeping. The dashed line is the failure path that lets traffic through.

diagram flow dir=TB title="One request through the limiter"
flow: client "Client request" user
flow: mw "rateLimit middleware" svc "Express, builds the key" focus
flow: redis "Redis" db "runs take.lua"
flow: ok "Token available?" decision
flow: app "Route handler" svc
flow: deny "429 + Retry-After" ext
flow: open "Fail open" ext "log + metric"
flow: client -> mw : request
flow: mw -> redis : EVALSHA
flow: redis -> ok : allowed, left, retry
flow: ok -> app : yes
flow: ok -> deny : no
flow: mw --> open : timeout or error
flow: open --> app : allow
flow: legend

## What a bucket does over time

Start full at 60. A burst of 60 at 1 s empties it. It refills 1 token per second, so by 30 s it holds 29. A second burst of 25 takes it to 4, and it creeps back to the 60 cap by 90 s.

data bucket = time,tokens; 0 s,60; 10 s,9; 20 s,19; 30 s,29; 40 s,13; 50 s,23; 60 s,33; 70 s,43; 80 s,53; 90 s,60; 100 s,60
chart line bucket x=time y=tokens target="60 capacity" mark="10 s burst of 60" mark="40 s burst of 25" title="Tokens left in one bucket" unit=" tokens"

## The four pieces of code

### 1. Limits config

The numbers live in one table. A route can override the default; anything not listed uses `default`.

```ts
// src/ratelimit/limits.ts
export const LIMITS: Record<string, Limit> = {
  default:         { capacity: 60,  refillPerSec: 1,  cost: 1 },
  "POST /search":  { capacity: 20,  refillPerSec: 0.5, cost: 1 },
  "POST /export":  { capacity: 5,   refillPerSec: 0.05, cost: 5 },
};
```

1. `capacity` is the burst size and the cap on stored tokens.
2. `refillPerSec` is the steady rate. It may be fractional: 0.05 is one token every 20 seconds.
3. `cost` is how many tokens one call takes. Export costs 5, so a full bucket allows exactly one export.

### 2. Building the key

```ts
// src/ratelimit/key.ts
export function bucketKey(req: Request): string {
  const who = req.auth?.tenantId ?? req.ip;
  const route = `${req.method} ${req.route.path}`;
  return `rl:{${who}}:${route}`;
}
```

1. Authenticated calls are limited per tenant, so every user in a company shares one bucket.
2. Anonymous calls fall back to the IP address.
3. The braces around `who` are a Redis Cluster hash tag. They keep all of one tenant's buckets on the same shard.

### 3. The Lua script

This is the heart of the limiter. It runs atomically on Redis, so no other command can interleave between the read and the write.

```lua
-- src/ratelimit/take.lua
local capacity = tonumber(ARGV[1])
local refill   = tonumber(ARGV[2])   -- tokens per second
local cost     = tonumber(ARGV[3])

local t   = redis.call('TIME')
local now = t[1] * 1000 + math.floor(t[2] / 1000)

local h      = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts')
local tokens = tonumber(h[1]) or capacity
local ts     = tonumber(h[2]) or now

tokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) * refill / 1000)

local allowed = 0
if tokens >= cost then
  tokens  = tokens - cost
  allowed = 1
end

redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now)
redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(capacity / refill * 1000) * 2)

local retry = 0
if allowed == 0 then retry = math.ceil((cost - tokens) / refill * 1000) end
return { allowed, math.floor(tokens), retry }
```

1. Lines 1 to 3 read the limit. Nothing is hard-coded in Lua.
2. Lines 5 and 6 take the time from Redis, not from the app server. App clocks drift; Redis has one clock.
3. Lines 8 to 10 load the bucket. A missing key means a brand-new caller, who starts full.
4. Line 12 is the refill: elapsed time times the rate, capped at capacity. Nobody runs a timer; refill is computed lazily on the next request.
5. Lines 14 to 18 decide. Tokens are taken only when there are enough for the full cost.
6. Line 21 sets the key to expire after twice the time needed to refill from empty. That is where the 120 s idle expiry comes from.
7. Lines 23 and 24 work out how long until enough tokens exist. That becomes `Retry-After`.

### 4. The middleware

```ts
// src/ratelimit/middleware.ts
export async function rateLimit(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const limit = LIMITS[`${req.method} ${req.route.path}`] ?? LIMITS.default;
  try {
    const [allowed, left, retryMs] = await withTimeout(
      redis.evalsha(TAKE_SHA, 1, bucketKey(req), limit.capacity, limit.refillPerSec, limit.cost),
      25,
    );
    res.setHeader("X-RateLimit-Remaining", left);
    if (!allowed) {
      res.setHeader("Retry-After", Math.ceil(retryMs / 1000));
      return res.status(429).json({ error: "rate_limited" });
    }
  } catch (err) {
    metrics.increment("ratelimit.fail_open");
    logger.warn({ err }, "rate limiter unavailable, allowing request");
  }
  next();
}
```

1. Picks the route's limit, or the default.
2. Calls the script by its SHA, with a 25 ms timeout so Redis trouble cannot stall the API.
3. Returns 429 with `Retry-After` in whole seconds when the script says no.
4. On any error or timeout it logs, bumps a metric and calls `next()`. The limiter fails open.

## Gotchas

!! Fail open means that if Redis is down, there is no rate limiting at all. Alert on `ratelimit.fail_open`, not just on 429s.

!! One tenant shares one bucket across all its users and API keys. A single noisy integration can lock out the rest of the company.

! `Retry-After` is rounded up to whole seconds, but the script reports milliseconds. A client that retries after exactly that many seconds will succeed; one that retries sooner will be refused again.

- **Idle buckets reset to full.** The key expires after 120 s (twice the time to refill from empty). A caller who pauses for two minutes comes back with a full bucket.
- **`EVALSHA` can fail with NOSCRIPT** after a Redis failover or restart. The client reloads the script and retries once; if that also fails you hit the fail-open path.
- **Hash tags concentrate load.** `{tenantId}` pins all of a tenant's buckets to one shard, so one very large tenant makes a hot shard.
- **Anonymous traffic is limited by IP.** Offices and mobile carriers share addresses, so legitimate users can be throttled together. Make sure `trust proxy` is set, or every request appears to come from the load balancer.
- **Tokens are fractional inside Redis** but `X-RateLimit-Remaining` is floored. A header of 0 can still mean 0.9 of a token.
- **Cost greater than capacity can never succeed.** Setting `cost: 5` with `capacity: 4` returns 429 forever and the script reports a retry time that never arrives.
